## Introduction
Under the global fossil fuel shortage and environmental crisis, the exploration of alternative energy sources is necessary to achieve sustainable development [1]. One of the most promising solutions is electrochemical water splitting to produce hydrogen based on the renewable energy [2]. Many researchers have also reported a tremendous number of electrocatalysts with excellent performance for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) [3-5]. However, the sluggish kinetics of oxygen evolution reaction （OER） which requires highly efficient and robust electrocatalysts usually hinders the development of water splitting system [6]. $\mathrm{Ir/Ru}$ based noble metal catalysts are considered the most active electrocatalysts for OER so far [7]. But the high cost and the scarcity of these catalysts limit their industrial applications. In this regard, highperformance, non-precious, and durable electrocatalysts for OER should be developed to reduce the high overpotentials and accelerate the reaction kinetics, leading to the commercial applications of electrocatalytic water splitting. Recently, earth abundant transition metal (such as Ni, Co, and Fe)-based catalysts have been reported as a promising class of electrocatalysts for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) [8] and OER, including oxides, hydroxides [9-12],sulfides [13],carbides [14],and nitrides [15]. For example, Tang et al. designed Fe-doped nickel telluride-nickel phosphide electrocatalyst, which exhibited excellent electrocatalytic performance [16].
Apart from the composition of catalysts, the surface structure of catalysts has a significant influence on the electrocatalytic performance [17]. Therefore, it is of great significance to regulate the structure and morphology of electrocatalysts which would guide the synthesis of highly efficient electrocatalyst [18]. Interface engineering is one of the most feasible approaches to effectively optimize the electrocatalytic activity [l9]. There are six effective interface  engineering  approaches for  rational  design of electrocatalysts, including morphology engineering, crystallographic tailoring, defect engineering, catalyst/electrolyte interface regulation, catalyst/substrate interface engineering, and heterostructure design [20,21]. Great efforts have been made for heterostructure design which has been widely employed to enhance electrocatalytic performance [22-25]. In particular, the synergisticeffect and  strong interfacial  interactions in heterostructures can improve charge transfer ability and induce an electronic coupling effect, finally optimizing the free energies of reaction steps and boosting the activity [26-30]. Recently, Feng and co-workers  suggested  that $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}{\mathrm{-Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}}$ heterostructure exhibited higher OER catalytic activity than the counterpart [31]. They found that the strong electronic interaction between the two phases $(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ ）has great contribution to the electrocatalytic performance. To date, metal phosphides also have been demonstrated to be promising electrocatalysts for OER. And theoretical calculations have predicted that binary transition metal phosphide shows an exotic performance for electrochemical OER [32]. Therefore, it is of great significance to achieve heterostructure design of binary metal phosphides. However, rational design of heterostructured metal phosphide electrocatalysts with high efficiency is still an ongoing challenge at the practical levels.
In this work, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were conducted to predict the catalytic performance of iron phosphide (FeP), cobalt phosphide(CoP), and Fe-Cophosphide heterostructures (denoted as FeP-CoP) for OER. Benefiting from the interface in heterostructures, both the Fe sites and $\mathrm{Co}$ sites in $_\mathrm{FeP-CoP}$ exhibit lower potential barriers than that of FeP or CoP, in which the Fe sites on the interface show the lowest potential barriers. Therefore, motivated by the results from DFT calculations, FeP-CoP heterostructures have been designed and synthesized by a metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) confinedphosphorization method. The FeP-CoP exhibits the lowest overpotential of $230~\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ compared with FeP $(470~\mathrm{~mV})$ and CoP( $340~\mathrm{\mV})$ ，which outperforms most of transition metal-based catalysts. And the FeP-CoP shows extraordinary long-term stability over $24\mathrm{h}$ .Our findings might open a new avenue for the application of metal phosphides heterostructures in sustainable energy conversion.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of MIL-88B nanorods
The MIL-88B nanorods were synthesized according to the previously reported hydrothermal method [33]. Firstly, $0.16\mathrm{~g~}$ F127 was dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, followed by the addition of 0.179 g $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under stirring for $^{\mathrm{~1~h~}}$ Subsequently, $0.5\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid was injected and the mixture was stirred for another $^{\mathrm{~1~h~}}$ After the addition of $0.06\mathrm{~g~}2\AA$ aminoterephthalic acid, the reaction mixture was stirred for another $2\mathrm{h}$ and subjected to hydrothermal treatment at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ The dark brown product was collected by centrifugation and washed with ethanol for several times.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MIL-88B@zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67) dual-MOFs composites
The as-prepared MIL-88B nanorods were dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol solution of polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP） $(0.5~\mathrm{g},M_{\mathrm{W}}=$ 58,000) and stirred at room temperature for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ 、The PVPfunctionalizedMIL-88Bnanorodswerecollected by centrifugation,washed by methanol for several times, and redispersed in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol. To synthesize MIL-88B@ZIF-67 dual-MOFs composites, $0.3~\mathrm{\mL}$ PVP-functionalized MIL-88B nanorods suspension, $2~\mathrm{mL}~100~\mathrm{mM}\stackrel{\cdot}{_{\cdot}}$ 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM) solution, 5 mL 10 mM $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ solution, and $2.7~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol were mixed to react at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ Finally, the product was collected by centrifugation, washed with methanol for several times, and dried overnight at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
## 2.3 Synthesis of FeP-CoP heterostructures
The obtained MIL-88B $@.$ ZIF-67 dual-MOFs composite was calcinated at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ in muffle furnace, and further annealed with $\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ under a flow of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ in a tube furnace. For comparison, FeP or CoP nanoparticles (NPs) were synthesized by a similar procedure based on MIL-88B or ZIF67, respectively.
## 2.4 Materials characterization
The morphologies of the samples were characterized by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S4800） and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL, JEM-2100). The compositions of the as-synthesized samples were characterized by energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) attached to the SEM. High-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) images and elemental mapping images were obtained by Talos F200x equipped with EDX. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD） was carried out on a Bruker D8 ADVANCE diffractometer with Cu $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Axis Supra) was used to characterize the valence states of the elements in the samples. The binding energy was calibrated with the measured C ls peak at $284.8~\mathrm{eV}$ Elemental compositions were analyzed by a SPECTRO ARCOS spectrometer using inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES).
## 2.5 Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical tests were performed on CHI 760E electrochemical workstation using a three-electrode system in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH at room temperature. $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode and graphite rod were employed as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. To prepare the working electrode, $20~\mathrm{~mg}$ of synthesized sample was mixed with $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion solution $(5~\mathrm{\wt.\%})$ ， $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol, and $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ isopropyl alcohol by ultrasound for $45\mathrm{min}$ . Then $90\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the ink was drop-coated on hydrophilic carbon paper $(1\ \mathrm{cm}\times1\ \mathrm{cm})$ with a mass loading of $3~{\mathrm{mg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV） measurements were conducted at a scan rate of $5\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . All the electrochemical measurements were conducted with $i R$ compensation. All potentials were referenced to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE): $E(\mathrm{RHE})\ =\ E(\mathrm{Hg/HgO})\ +\ 0.098\ +\ 0.059\ \times\ \mathrm{pH}$ The electrochemical double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(}C_{\mathrm{dl}}\mathrm{)}$ was evaluated by cyclic voltammetry (CV） curves measured at non-faradaic potentials range from of 1.34 to $1.44\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE) according to the following equation: $C_{\mathrm{dl}}{=}I_{\mathrm{c}}/\nu,$ where $C_{\mathrm{d}\mathrm{b}}\ I_{\mathrm{c}},$ and $\nu$ are the doublelayer capacitance, charging current, and scan rate, respectively. Specifically, the value of $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ was estimated by plotting current density variation $(\Delta j\ (j_{\mathrm{a}}\ -\ j_{\mathrm{c}}))$ versus the scan rate at a certain potential, while the half of the slope was $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ ，Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were tested from l to $10^{6}\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at the open-circuit voltage. The long-term stabilities of FeP NPs, CoP NPs, and FeP-CoP heterostructure were performed by chronoamperometry (CA） at the potentials of 1.7l, l.62, and $1.55\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE), respectively.
