## 3.2 Design and synthesis of heterostructure
Inspired by the DFT theoretical predictions, FeP-CoP heterostructures were synthesized via a two-step method as illustrated in Fig.2. In a typical synthesis, MIL-88B nanorods were first synthesized through a hydrothermal method [33]. Then the MIL-88B nanorods (Fig.S2 in the ESM) were mixed with cobalt nitrate and 2-MIM at room temperature to produce MIL88B@ZIF-67. Finally, unique FeP-CoP heterostructures were obtained by chemically converting MIL-88B@ZIF-67 into Fe-Co mixed metal phosphides by thermal treatment with $\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ SEM images (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)) show the morphology of the asobtained MIL-88B@ZIF-67 composites. Uniform MIL-88B nanorods are evenly distributed on  ZIF-67 polyhedrons, compared with the pure ZIF-67 NPs (Fig.S3(a) in the ESM). The TEM image (Fig. 3(c)) unambiguously reveals that the ZIF-67 NPs contain many MIL-88B nanorods which are distributed inside the polyhedrons. The XRD patterns (Fig.3(d)) reveal that the MIL88B@ZIF-67 exhibits a similar XRD pattern to that of ZIF-67. In addition, EDX spectrum (Fig.S4 in the ESM) shows the existence of Fe and Co elements, evidencing the successful incorporation of MIL-88B nanorods in ZIF-67 NPs. And the elemental mapping images of the MIL-88B $@$ ZIF-67 (Fig.S5 in the ESM) reveal the uniform distribution of Fe and Co elements. The ratio of MIL-88B nanorods in each MIL-88B $@.$ ZIF-67 could be controlled by tuning the amount of MIL-88B nanorods added to the reaction system (Fig. S6 in the ESM).
To achieve sufficient phosphorization, the MIL-88B $@$ ZIF-67 was firstly oxidized at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ in air (Fig. S7 in the ESM). Subsequently, the FeP-CoP heterostructures were synthesized through phosphorization of the oxidized MIL-88B@ZIF-67 with $\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{2}}$ at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. After the phosphorization treatment, SEM images (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)) show the morphology of rhombic dodecahedron is maintained. But the size of rhombic dodecahedron is reduced because of the shrinkage during the thermal treatment. The XRD patterns (Fig 5(a)） confirm the products consist of FeP (JCPDS no. 39-0809) and CoP (JCPDS no. 29-0497). The $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ atomic ratio is 1:1.6 according to the ICP-OES results (Table S1 in the ESM), which is close to the EDX results (1:1.7) of FeP-CoP (Fig.S8 in the ESM). In addition, phosphorization of the MIL-88B and ZIF-67 was also carried out under the same conditions. In the case of MIL-88B, SEM image (Fig.S9 in the ESM) indicates the structure of nanorods has been destroyed after phosphorization. The XRD patterns (Fig. 5(a)) reveal that the diffraction peaks can be indexed to FeP (JCPDS no. 39-0809). For ZIF-67,Fig.S10 in the ESM shows the morphology can be maintained after phosphorization. According to the XRD patterns (Fig. 5(a)), the obtained product is denoted as CoP.
The structure of FeP-CoP is further examined by TEM (Fig
4(c)). The rhombic dodecahedral features can be clearly observed. Furthermore, the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Fig. 4(d)) shows that the (120) crystal plane of FeP (with lattice plane of $0.250~\mathrm{nm}$ ）couples with the (102) crystal plane of CoP (with lattice plane of $0.244\ \mathrm{nm}$ ),which is consistent with the XRD patterns. And these results confirm the existence of the heterointerface of FeP-CoP, which indicates the successful establishment of heterostructures. The HAADF-STEM image and elemental mapping images suggest the uniform distribution of Co, Fe, and P on the FeP-CoP heterostructure (Figs. 4(e)-4(h)). XPS has been used to measure the elemental compositions and valence states. XPS survey spectrum (Fig.S1l in the ESM) of FeP-CoP confirms the existence of Fe, Co, P, C, and O elements. The O element may arise from the superficial oxidation of FeP-CoP as a result of air contact [40]. And ${\mathrm{Co-O}}$ and Fe-O bonds have also been observed in the XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ and Fe 2p. In the high resolution XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}$ (Fig. 5(b)), the peaks at 778.6 (Co $\mathrm{2p}_{3/2}\mathrm{)}$ and $793.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Co $\mathrm{2p_{1/2}}\mathrm{,}$ are attributed to Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ in CoP. The peaks at 707.4 and $720.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ in Fe 2p can be assigned to Fe $\mathrm{2p}_{3/2}$ and $\boldsymbol{2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}}$ in FeP. The P species with binding energies of 129.7 and $130.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ boned to Fe or $\mathrm{Co}$ (Fig. 5(d)). XPS spectra of CoP (Fig. S12 in the ESM) and FeP (Fig. S13 in the ESM) have also been provided. The binding energy of the Fe $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{p}}_{3/2}$ (Fe-P) exhibits a positive shift of $0.4\:\mathrm{eV}$ than that of pure FeP, suggesting an increased electron density of the Fe in FeP-CoP as compared to pure FeP, which confirms the electron transfer in the heterostructure.
## 3.3 OER catalytic performance
A typical three-electrode system was applied to study the electrocatalytic OER performance of the FeP NPs, CoP NPs, and FeP-CoP heterostructures in $1.0\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ solution. Figure 6(a) shows the polarization curves with iR correction. The as-prepared FeP-CoP heterostructures catalyst shows the highest OER catalytic performance with the lowest overpotential of $230~\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ 、outperforming other samples of FeP with $470~\mathrm{mV},$ CoP with $340~\mathrm{mV};$ and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ with $260~\mathrm{mV}$ which is also superior to the performance of many other $\mathrm{Co}$ -Fe-based electrocatalysts (Table S2 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 6(b), the kinetic behaviors of the FeP NPs, CoP NPs, and FeP-CoP heterostructures are further explored. The Tafel analysis (Fig.6(b)) of FeP-CoP heterostructures shows an improved reaction kinetic pathway with the smallest slope of $90.3\ \mathrm{\mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ ， as compared to the Tafel slopes of FeP NPs $\left(137\mathrm{mV\cdotdec}^{-1}\right)$ and CoP NPs ( $\mathrm{114mV{\cdot}d e c^{-1}},$ . To further study the charge transfer kinetics, the measurements of EIS were performed (Fig.Sl4 in the ESM). The Nyquist plots show a semicircle, suggesting the charge transfer process. Among all the samples, the $\mathrm{FeP-CoP}$ heterostructures exhibit the smallest charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , implying the fast electron transfer kinetics.
In addition, the electrochemical surface area (ECSA) has been adopted to explore the active surface area in OER (Fig. S15 in the ESM). ECSA can be evaluated by $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ 、The ECSA value of FeP-CoP heterostructures is determined to $418.6~\mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ as compared to FeP NPs $(1.08\mathrm{~\mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})$ and CoPNPs $(290.8\mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})$ ）(Fig.6(c))， indicating that the FeP-CoP heterostructures have the highest number of exposed active sites.
And stability of the electrocatalysts is also critical in OER performance. The stability is tested by CA method. The FeP-CoP heterostructures display outstanding durability at $20\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ with a negligible current loss (Fig. 6(d)). However, it could be clearly observed that the current densities of FeP NPs and CoP NPs decreased significantly during the CA test (Fig.Sl6 in the ESM). Apart from electrochemical characterization, the morphology of FeP-CoP heterostructures after the CA test was characterized by SEM (Fig.Sl7 in the ESM), indicating the original morphology could be maintained. And the elemental mapping images of the FeP-CoP heterostructures (Fig. S18 in the ESM) also indicate the unchanged elemental compositions. XRD patterns (Fig.S19 in the ESM) of FeP-CoP heterostructures after the CA test also confirm that the crystal structure remains the same after the long-term durability test. Subsequently, XPS measurements (Fig.S20 in the ESM) of the used FeP-CoP heterostructures indicate that Fe and Co cations on the surface are partially oxidized to FeOOH and CoOOH, which is consistent with the reported mechanism [4l,42]. All these results demonstrate that the FeP-CoP heterostructures show superior electrocatalytic OER performance as well as excellent stability. The higher OER performance of our FeP-CoP heterostructures might be attributed to the following reasons. First, the interface engineering could induce the synergistic effect and strong interfacial interactions in heterostructures which improve charge transfer ability and induce an electronic coupling effect [43]. Second, the unique FeP-CoP heterostructures lead to a large active surface and effective charge transfer.  Also, the heterostructures  possess  much  higher  robustness  which contributes to the higher stability as compared to pure FeP or CoP.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, motivated by the results from DFT calculations, we have developed a dual-MOF confined-phosphorization approach for the synthesis of FeP-CoP heterostructures. Interestingly, as predicted by DFT calculations, the potential barriers of Fe sites at the interface of heterostructures are lower than that of Fe sites in FeP, Co sites in CoP, or Co sites in $\mathrm{FeP-CoP}$ The electrocatalytic performance of the obtained FeP-CoP heterostructures is in good agreement with the theoretical predictions. The FeP-CoP exhibits the lowest overpotential of $230~\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ 、compared with FeP $\mathrm{470~mV},$ ）andCoP( $340~\mathrm{mV})$ ， Additionally, the FeP-CoP heterostructures show better long-term stability. The enhanced OER performance might be attributed to the interaction between FeP and CoP. This work will be of fundamental significance in the design of heterostructure materials
for water splitting.
